2.7 Theorem A composition of linear maps is represented by the matrix product
of the representatives.
Proof This argument generalizes Example 2.2. Let h: V →W and g: W →X
be represented by H and G with respect to bases B ⊂V, C ⊂W, and D ⊂X, of
sizes n, r, and m. For any ⃗v ∈V the k-th component of RepC( h(⃗v) ) is
hk,1v1 + · · · + hk,nvn
and so the i-th component of RepD( g ◦h (⃗v) ) is this.
gi,1 · (h1,1v1 + · · · + h1,nvn) + gi,2 · (h2,1v1 + · · · + h2,nvn)
+ · · · + gi,r · (hr,1v1 + · · · + hr,nvn)
Distribute and regroup on the v’s.
= (gi,1h1,1 + gi,2h2,1 + · · · + gi,rhr,1) · v1
+ · · · + (gi,1h1,n + gi,2h2,n + · · · + gi,rhr,n) · vn
Finish by recognizing that the coeﬃcient of each vj
gi,1h1,j + gi,2h2,j + · · · + gi,rhr,j
matches the deﬁnition of the i, j entry of the product GH.
QED
