By the ﬁrst line ⃗κ3 ̸= ⃗0, since ⃗β3 isn’t in the span [⃗β1, ⃗β2] and therefore by the
inductive hypothesis it isn’t in the span [⃗κ1,⃗κ2]. By the second line ⃗κ3 is in
the span of the ﬁrst three ⃗β’s. Finally, the calculation below shows that ⃗κ3 is
orthogonal to ⃗κ1.
⃗κ1 • ⃗κ3 = ⃗κ1 • ␀ ⃗β3 −proj[⃗κ1](⃗β3) −proj[⃗κ2](⃗β3)

= ⃗κ1 • ␀⃗β3 −proj[⃗κ1](⃗β3)

−⃗κ1 • proj[⃗κ2](⃗β3)
= 0
(Here is the diﬀerence with the i = 2 case: as happened for i = 2 the ﬁrst term
is 0 because this projection is orthogonal, but here the second term in the second
line is 0 because ⃗κ1 is orthogonal to ⃗κ2 and so is orthogonal to any vector in
the line spanned by ⃗κ2.) A similar check shows that ⃗κ3 is also orthogonal to ⃗κ2.
QED
