Any vector with a ﬁrst component of 1 would be aﬀected in the same way as
⃗u: it would slide up by 2. And any vector with a ﬁrst component of 2 would
slide up 4, as was ⃗v. That is, the transformation represented by Ci,j(k) aﬀects
vectors depending on their i-th component.
Another way to see this point is to consider the action of this map on the
unit square. In the next picture, vectors with a ﬁrst component of 0, such as the
origin, are not pushed vertically at all but vectors with a positive ﬁrst component
slide up. Here, all vectors with a ﬁrst component of 1, the entire right side of
the square, slide to the same extent. In general, vectors on the same vertical
line slide by the same amount, by twice their ﬁrst component. The resulting
shape has the same base and height as the square (and thus the same area) but
the right angle corners are gone.
