2.15 Lemma If a space has a t-string basis then the index of nilpotency of t
equals the length of the longest string in that basis.
Proof Let the space have a basis of t-strings and let t’s index of nilpotency
be k. Then tk sends any vector to ⃗0, and that must include including the vector
starting any string. So each string in the string basis has length at most k.
Now instead suppose that the space has a t-string basis B where all of the
strings are shorter than length k. Because t has the index of nilpotency k, there
is a ⃗v such that tk−1(⃗v) ̸= ⃗0. Represent ⃗v as a linear combination of elements
from B and apply tk−1. We are supposing that tk−1 maps each element of B
to ⃗0. It therefore maps each term in the linear combination to ⃗0, contradicting
that it does not map ⃗v to ⃗0.
QED
