As a result, the parameters we get depend on the interval we observe. To see
how much effect this has on the predictions, we can extend SimulateResults
to use intervals of observation with different start and end dates. My imple-
mentation is in timeseries.py.
Figure 12.7 shows the result for the medium quality category. The lightest
gray area shows a confidence interval that includes uncertainty due to sam-
pling error, random variation, and variation in the interval of observation.
The model based on the entire interval has positive slope, indicating that
prices were increasing. But the most recent interval shows signs of decreasing
prices, so models based on the most recent data have negative slope. As a
result, the widest predictive interval includes the possibility of decreasing
prices over the next year.


